      "title": "FIFO Matching Engine Physics & Level 2 vs Level 3 Visibility",
      "say": [
        "On major modern electronic equities and futures exchanges—such as NASDAQ, the New York Stock Exchange (NYSE), BATS/Cboe, and the Chicago Mercantile Exchange (CME)—limit order books operate strictly under the Price-Time Priority matching rule, universally known as First-In, First-Out (FIFO).",
        "Under FIFO priority, when an aggressive market order or marketable limit order crosses the bid-ask spread, the matching engine matches it against resting limit orders sitting at the best price level in the exact chronological order in which those resting orders were accepted. If there are 50,000 shares resting at the Best Bid price of $100.00, and your algorithmic trading strategy submits a buy limit order for 1,000 shares, your order is placed at the very tail of the queue. All 50,000 shares resting ahead of you must be either executed or cancelled before your order receives even a single share of fill.",
        "To model this queue progression accurately, quantitative engineers must understand the fundamental difference between Level 2 (Aggregated Market By Price - MBP) and Level 3 (Individual Market By Order - MBO) market data feeds.",
        "In Level 3 data feeds (such as NASDAQ TotalView-ITCH or CME MDP 3.0), the exchange disseminates granular, order-by-order protocol messages: every order is assigned a unique 64-bit Order Reference Number (`OrderReferenceID`). ITCH broadcasts an `Order Add` message when an order arrives, an `Order Cancel` or `Order Delete` message with the specific `OrderReferenceID` when shares are revoked, and an `Order Executed` message when shares trade. With Level 3 feeds, tracking your queue position is mathematically trivial: you record the list of order IDs resting ahead of you, and whenever an order cancellation or trade packet arrives matching one of those IDs, you decrement the exact quantity resting ahead.",
        "However, consuming and parsing Level 3 ITCH feeds requires massive networking infrastructure, expensive exchange proprietary feed licensing fees (costing tens of thousands of dollars per month per venue), and substantial FPGA processing power. Furthermore, many exchanges and liquidity pools (such as European venues under MiFID II, consolidated SIP feeds, retail brokerage APIs, and secondary ATS dark pools) do NOT provide Level 3 feeds! They only disseminate Level 2 data feeds.",
        "In Level 2 data, individual order identities are completely invisible: the exchange only broadcasts aggregated price levels: `Best Bid: $100.00, Total Size: 50,000 shares`. When the size suddenly drops from 50,000 to 45,000 shares, the trading engine receives a notification that 5,000 shares disappeared, but Level 2 provides ZERO information regarding whether those 5,000 shares were cancelled from ahead of your order, cancelled from behind your order, or executed against an incoming market order!",
        "This lack of visibility creates the fundamental quantitative challenge of Queue Position Estimation: developing probabilistic and deterministic models to reconstruct your order's position in the FIFO queue using only aggregated Level 2 volume deltas and tape prints.",
        "In this part, we implement a C++ Order Book Representation Engine that models FIFO queues, contrasts Level 2 aggregated depth against Level 3 individual order queues, and establishes the foundational state variables for queue position tracking."
      ],
      "example": "Think of waiting in a single-file line at airport security. If you have an overhead camera showing every passenger's face (Level 3 ITCH), you can see exactly which person steps out of line to buy coffee. But if you only have a sensor reporting total line weight (Level 2), you only know the line got lighter, not who left.",
      "code": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <cstdint>\n#include <iomanip>\n\nstruct QueueStateL2 {\n    double price;\n    uint32_t totalVolume;\n    uint32_t volumeAhead;\n    uint32_t myOrderSize;\n    uint64_t submissionTimestampNs;\n};\n\nclass FIFOQueueModelL2 {\nprivate:\n    QueueStateL2 m_state;\n\npublic:\n    FIFOQueueModelL2(double price, uint32_t existingDepth, uint32_t mySize, uint64_t timestamp) {\n        m_state.price = price;\n        m_state.totalVolume = existingDepth + mySize;\n        m_state.volumeAhead = existingDepth; // Under FIFO, all prior resting depth is ahead\n        m_state.myOrderSize = mySize;\n        m_state.submissionTimestampNs = timestamp;\n    }\n\n    // Inspect initial queue allocation\n    void displayQueue() const {\n        std::cout << \"--- Level 2 FIFO Limit Order Queue Snapshot ---\\n\";\n        std::cout << \"Price Level:        $\" << std::fixed << std::setprecision(2) << m_state.price << \"\\n\";\n        std::cout << \"Volume Ahead (Q_a): \" << m_state.volumeAhead << \" shares\\n\";\n        std::cout << \"My Order Size:      \" << m_state.myOrderSize << \" shares\\n\";\n        std::cout << \"Total Queue Depth:  \" << m_state.totalVolume << \" shares\\n\";\n        double queuePct = (static_cast<double>(m_state.volumeAhead) / m_state.totalVolume) * 100.0;\n        std::cout << \"Queue Position:     \" << std::setprecision(1) << queuePct << \"% behind front\\n\";\n    }\n\n    uint32_t getVolumeAhead() const { return m_state.volumeAhead; }\n    uint32_t getTotalVolume() const { return m_state.totalVolume; }\n};\n\nint inspectFIFOQueueBasics() {\n    // 40,000 shares already resting at $100.00; we submit 2,500 shares\n    FIFOQueueModelL2 queue(100.00, 40000, 2500, 1600000000000ULL);\n    queue.displayQueue();\n\n    return 0;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    return inspectFIFOQueueBasics();\n}",
