  "goal": "In the 2000s, trading firms laid fiber optic cables through mountains between Chicago (CME) and New Jersey (NASDAQ/NYSE), achieving round-trip times of 13 milliseconds. But in glass fiber, light travels at approximately 200,000 km/s\u2014only two-thirds the speed of light in a vacuum! By 2011, HFT firms began erecting microwave radio towers across the Midwest. Through open air, microwave signals travel at 99.9% of the speed of light in a vacuum! Microwave slashed round-trip times to under 8 milliseconds, rendering fiber obsolete for price discovery. Today, you will analyze the physics, economics, and architectural engineering of Global Microwave Networks. You will calculate geodesic line-of-sight paths, model atmospheric weather fade (rain attenuation), and design hybrid multi-carrier failover routers with microsecond switching that keep your trading models connected at the speed of light!",
  "minutes": 38,
  "recap": "In Day 57, we deconstructed the 2010 Flash Crash, modeled quote-to-trade ratios and phantom liquidity, and implemented real-time anti-spoofing filters. Today, we conquer planetary physics, line-of-sight microwave links, and weather-resilient failover routers.",
